  "data": {
    "title": "Spontaneous eye movements during eyes-open rest reduce resting-state-network modularity by increasing visual-sensorimotor connectivity",
    "text": "Title: Spontaneous eye movements during eyes-open rest reduce resting-state-network modularity by increasing visual-sensorimotor connectivity\n\nFunctional Connectivity Networks\nTo create functional connectivity networks, we used a resting-state functional connectivity parcellation based on 500 ROIs (Schaefer et al., 2018). We spatially translated this parcellation into each participant’s individual space and limited ROIs to gray matter. We extracted the mean time series from each ROI.\n\nWe examined the network features after thresholding the connectivity matrices at 12 sparsity levels: 30%, 20%, and 1–10%. We generally used nonbinarized connections maintaining the original weights, with the following exceptions: for node degree we used binarized values.\n\nFrom each participant’s resting-state network we derived the following metrics: node degree, strength, clustering coefficient, transitivity, assortativity, efficiency, number of communities, betweenness centrality, and modularity. These were calculated using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox (Rubinov & Sporns, 2010).\n\nWe tested which of these parameters differed as a result of the EO-EPI removal procedure using paired-sample t tests. We defined a robust result as one that was statistically significant across all 12 levels of sparsity thresholding."
  },
